pdated November 14, 2026.</em></p><p><strong>Louisiana law sets out when a person occupying land can claim title by adverse possession, and how an owner can remove someone who has no right to be there.</strong></p><h2>What the official sources say</h2><ul><li>Acquisitive prescription of immovables: ten years&#x27; possession in good faith with just title; thirty years&#x27; uninterrupted possession without title or good faith (stated for apparent servitudes, which follow the immovable rules). (La. C.C. Art. 742 (refers to Arts. 3473, 3486); <a href='https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=110937' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer noopener nofollow'>source</a>)</li><li>Owner evicting an occupant (non-tenant) after the purpose of occupancy has ceased must deliver written notice to vacate allowing five days. (La. C.C.P. Art. 4702; <a href='https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=112074' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer noopener nofollow'>source</a>)</li><li>If the occupant does not comply, owner may cite the occupant summarily by rule to show cause why possession should not be delivered. (La. C.C.P. Art. 4731(A); <a href='https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=112078' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer noopener nofollow'>source</a>)</li></ul><div class=\"chb-cta\" style=\"margin:32px 0;border-radius:10px;background:linear-gradient(120deg,#0F1C2E 0%,#16293F 100%);padding:28px 32px;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:20px;flex-wrap:wrap;color:#fff;\"><div style=\"max-width:600px;\"><div style=\"font-size:12px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:2px;color:#E0A83C;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:6px;\">Selling in Louisiana?</div><div style=\"font-weight:800;font-size:20px;line-height:1.35;color:#fff;\">We buy Louisiana houses as-is, with no commission, no repairs and no closing costs to you.</div></div><a href=\"#\" onclick=\"var f=document.querySelector('iframe[data-form-id]');if(f){f.scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth',block:'center'});return false;}\" style=\"flex-shrink:0;padding:14px 24px;border-radius:8px;background:#E0A83C;color:#16203A;font-size:14px;font-weight:800;text-decoration:none;white-space:nowrap;\">Get My Cash Offer &rarr;</a></div><h2>Louisiana Squatters Rights and Adverse Possession FAQ</h2><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Can a squatter take ownership of my Louisiana house?</h3><p> Check the statute and talk to a Louisiana attorney about your facts.</p><div class='wsp-related'><div class='wsp-related-grid'><div class='wsp-related-card'><a href='https://cashhomebuyersca.com/louisiana-landlord-tenant-law-tenant-rights/'>Louisiana Landlord Tenant Law and Tenant Rights</a><p>Official sources.</p></div><div class='wsp-related-card'><a href='https://cashhomebuyersca.com/louisiana-eviction-notice/'>Louisiana Eviction Notice: Types and Days</a><p>Official sources.</p></div><div class='wsp-related-card'><a href='https://cashhomebuyersca.com/louisiana-guides-by-city-and-county/'>Louisiana guides by city and county</a><p>Every local guide we publish.</p></div><div class='wsp-related-card'><a href='https://cashhomebuyersca.com/we-buy-houses-in-louisiana/'>We buy houses in Louisiana</a><p>How a cash offer works.</p></div></div></div><h2>Sources</h2><ul><li><a href='https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=110937' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer noopener nofollow'>La. C.C. Art. 742 (refers to Arts. 3473, 3486)</a></li><li><a href='https://legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=112074' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer noopener nofollow'>La. C.C.P. Art. 4702</a></li><li><a href='https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/Law.aspx?d=112078' target='_blank' rel='noreferrer noopener nofollow'>La. C.C.P. Art. 4731(A)</a></li></ul><p><em>General information for Louisiana Squatters Rights and Adverse Possession, not legal or tax advice. Confirm details with the official office or a licensed professional.</em></p><script type=\"application/ld+json\">{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\", \"@type\": \"FAQPage\", \"mainEntity\": [{\"@type\": \"Question\", \"name\": \"Can a squatter take ownership of my Louisiana house?\", \"acceptedAnswer\": {\"@type\": \"Answer\", \"text\": \"Only by meeting every requirement of the adverse possession statute cited above for the full period. Check the statute and talk to a Louisiana attorney about your facts.\"}}]}</script><script type=\"application/ld+json\">{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\", \"@type\": \"BreadcrumbList\", \"itemListElement\": [{\"@type\": \"ListItem\", \"position\": 1, \"name\": \"Home\", \"item\": \"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/\"}, {\"@type\": \"ListItem\", \"position\": 2, \"name\": \"Louisiana\", \"item\": \"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/we-buy-houses-in-louisiana/\"}, {\"@type\": \"ListItem\", \"position\": 3, \"name\": \"Louisiana Squatters Rights and Adverse Possession\", \"item\": \"https://cashhomebuyersca.com/louisiana-squatters-rights-adverse-possession/\"}]}</script></div>\n<!-- /wp:html -->", "status": "publish", "rank_math_title": "Louisiana Squatters Rights and Adverse Possession", "rank_math_description": "Louisiana squatters rights and adverse possession: what the statutes say and how owners remove occupants or sell. Official sources.", "rank_math_focus_keyword": "louisiana squatters rights"}, {"slug": "louisiana-landlord-tenant-law-tenant-rights", "title": "Louisiana Landlord Tenant Law and Tenant Rights: Rules for Selling a Rental", "content": "<!-- wp:html -->\n<style>.wsp-hero{position:relative;margin:0 calc(-50vw + 50%);padding:5rem 1.5rem;background-image:none;background:linear-gradient(170deg,#12293f 0%,#0c1c2e 46%,#060c16 100%);overflow:hidden;}.wsp-hero-inner{max-width:1216px;margin:0 auto;display:grid;grid-template-columns:1.1fr 1fr;gap:2.5rem;align-items:center;}.wsp-hero-left{text-align:center;}.wsp-hero-left h1{color:#fff;font-size:2.6rem;font-weight:800;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0 1rem;text-shadow:1px 1px 2px rgba(0,0,0,.3);}.wsp-hero-left h2{color:#fff;font-weight:300;font-size:1.3rem;margin:0 0 1rem;text-shadow:1p